Subject: Feedback on ILT Schedule Feasibility and Course Duration


For the Instructor-Led Training (ILT) delivery of AI267 I wanted to share a few observations regarding its feasibility and effective delivery.

Looking at the current ILT breakdown in InstructorCentralWIki, the course content totals 22 hours and 5 minutes spread across 4 days, averaging roughly 5.5 to 6.5 hours of pure instruction time per day.

If we attempt to compress or deliver this content within a 3-day window,assuming standard 8-hour training days with a mandatory 1-hour break (lunch/rest), the effective teaching time is 7 hours per day, totaling 21 hours over 3 days.This leaves virtually zero buffer for Q&A, lab troubleshooting, or natural discussion breaks.

Specifically, heavy and complex topics will be difficult for students to thoroughly absorb under this compressed timeline especially following chapters, 
Chapter 3: Fundamentals of Model Serving (3h 06m)
Chapter 5: Monitoring AI Models (3h 03m)
Chapter 7: Advanced AI Pipelines Development and Experiments (2h 27m combined across sections)
Chapter 8: Gen AI Model Optimization and Evaluation (2h 03m)
Chapter 9: Building GenAI Applications (2h 58m)
Given the technical weight of these sections—particularly Chapters 3, 5, 7, 8, and 9—rushing through them in a 3-day format will impact learning quality and engagement.
So to ensure a high-quality learning experience and allowing sufficient time for hands-on exercises and troubleshooting, I strongly recommend sticking to a 4-day delivery schedule rather than trying to fit it into 3 days.

